104:## 7. Pre-registered hypotheses and decision rules
106:**Estimands and intervals.** HAA: per-query paired differences `Δ_{s,q} = e_{X,s,q} − e_{Y,s,q}` for fine-tuning seed `s` and test query `q` of one room and metric; point estimate = mean over `(s, q)`; intervals from `tools/exp06_bootstrap.py`, an exp_06-owned, **alpha-aware** percentile bootstrap implementing exp_02's two schemes (query-cluster: the rows of one query across seeds resampled together; two-way: queries and seeds resampled with multiplicity weights) with exp_02's frozen conventions — `np.random.default_rng(seed)`, float64, historical row order and `np.unique` label order, query-bootstrap draws first then two-way draws from the same advancing generator, multiplicity products with weighted denominators, NumPy **linear** percentiles — `n_boot = 10 000` for nominal 95 % (must reproduce all eleven canonical exp_02 cells exactly — regression test; the round-2 review confirmed this is achievable, max abs diff 0.0) and `n_boot = 50 000` for adjusted tails. **Convergence** (per interval used for a verdict): `paired_compare.convergence` semantics — maximum endpoint movement between bootstrap seeds 0 and 1 divided by the seed-0 interval width ≤ 0.10, zero-width intervals refused; on failure `n_boot` is quadrupled once, and if it still fails the verdict is withheld (reported as *not converged*). Verdicts use the two-way interval. Simulation: exp_04's estimand `ρ = [mean_q(mean_s e_{C,s,q}) − mean_q(mean_s e_{B,s,q})] / mean_q(mean_s e_{B,s,q})` (queries averaged over the five evaluation seeds first; the ratio recomputed inside each paired query-level resample; room-cluster interval reported), one-sided 97.5 % upper bound, via `tools/paired_compare.{five_seed_mean, cell_mask, rho_bootstrap, h1_verdict}`.
108:**Paired cohort and invalidity policy.** For each cell, the cohort is the set of **distinct queries** with finite values in every compared run (both arms, all seeds); a query is invalid for an arm if any of that arm's seeds has a non-finite value; excluded counts are reported per arm and seed. A decision-bearing verdict (**H1 and H1b**) is **void** if the treatment arm's invalid-query count exceeds the comparator's by more than 1 % of the room's test size (4 queries for the hallway), or if the cohort is smaller than 99 % of the test split. Void verdicts are reported as such; they are never replaced by a subset analysis.
113:- **H2 (descriptive screen, not a parity claim)** — all 11 room × metric cells for C − A: nominal and Bonferroni-adjusted two-way intervals (two-sided level 1 − 0.05/11, tails at 0.05/22 = 0.00227 and 0.99773, ≈ 114 draws per tail at `n_boot = 50 000`); cells with adjusted lower bound > 0 are reported as **detected harm**, cells with adjusted upper bound < 0 as **detected improvement**, all others as **no detected difference** — never as "non-inferior". The positive conclusion of the experiment rests on H1 together with H1b (see the overall reading).
117:**Overall reading.** The positive conclusion is scoped to the **hallway C50 deficit**: "the orientation-conditioned cylindrical pipeline removes the hallway C50 deficit" iff G1 passes and both H1 and H1b hold (neither void, both converged). H2 describes every other cell (detected harm / improvement / no detected difference); H3 states whether the pipeline costs simulated accuracy. C − F is conditional on unpaired pretraining realisations (C is a new training run) and is labelled so. Anything else is reported as found; margins and rules change only through §10a amendments logged before the affected runs.
